### Extending Low Latency Service Across the Internet
* **Presenter:** Fatih Berkay Sarke
* **Slides:** [Extending Low Latency Service Across the Internet](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iccrg-extending-low-latency-service-across-the-internet-00)
* **Discussion:**
  * Fatih Berkay Sarke presented work addressing L4S performance degradation when transient bottlenecks occur outside the customer access link (e.g., in transit networks or peering points).
  * Analysis of CAIDA backbone traces (100 Gbps links) showed sharp packet inter-arrival clustering at serialization times, indicating transient upstream queuing even at moderate average utilization.
  * **Proposed Architecture:**
    * *Intra-domain:* Uses SRv6 (Segment Routing over IPv6) and local DSCP markings to steer L4S traffic onto isolated low-latency paths within a domain.
    * *Inter-domain:* Uses BGP Community signaling to advertise L4S support across transit ASes, allowing senders to select end-to-end L4S-capable paths.
    * *Co-existence Bottlenecks:* Replaces complex DualQ AQMs in high-speed core routers with rate-capped priority queues to protect L4S flows without heavy per-flow state.
* **Q&A and Chat Highlights:**
  * *Deployability & Scope:* Lars Eggert asked whether moving beyond the access link bottleneck assumption undermines the simplicity of L4S. Vidhi Goel noted that while access networks yield the primary L4S gains today, core handling provides additional protection against transient core queues.
  * *Path stability and BGP signaling:* Stefan Kerschbaumer questioned the frequency and convergence of BGP Community updates under route churn.
  * *L4S in Core Networks:* In chat, Sebastian Moeller and Martin Duke discussed whether priority queuing without DualQ queue-coupling in transit networks could be abused, while Ingemar Johansson noted transit networks typically have high bandwidth and shallow time-buffers.

