### Measuring Service Congestion, Sharing, and Pacing in a Residential Access Network
* **Presenter:** Bruce Spang
* **Slides:** [Measuring Service Congestion, Sharing, and Pacing in a Residential Access Network](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iccrg-measuring-service-congestion-sharing-and-pacing-in-a-residential-access-network-00)
* **Discussion:**
  * Bruce Spang presented passive measurement data collected via an aggregation switch tap across 33 Columbia University residential buildings (~1,500 units).
  * **Congestion Findings:** Despite low average building link utilization (95th percentile ~13%), individual units experienced high 90th-percentile weekly loss (>2%) and significant access RTT delay (>30 ms added delay). Sub-second analysis revealed extreme microburstiness (1-10 ms intervals exceeding 1 Gbps capacity).
  * **Queue Sharing:** Using an *overlapping packets* metric (packets whose round-trip window overlaps in time), the study found that 60% of packets only overlap with packets from the same TCP flow, 80% overlap only with the same service, and only 4% experience cross-service queue sharing.
  * **Application-Informed Pacing (SAMI Experiment):** Applied pacing to YouTube and Netflix video streams (comprising ~20% of network volume) over a multi-week toggled deployment.
  * **Results:** Video pacing reduced overall residential access network packet loss by ~50% and access RTT by 30–40% for paced flows, with zero negative impact on video quality or stall metrics. Overlapping non-video traffic also saw a ~29% loss reduction and ~36% latency reduction.
* **Q&A Highlights:**
  * *Seasonality & Traffic Patterns:* Stefan Kerschbaumer asked about academic calendar biases. Spang clarified October data was chosen due to stable student populations and consistent hourly traffic distributions.
  * *Application Metrics:* Martin Duke and Vidhi Goel asked if application-layer metrics (re-buffers/stalls) were impacted. Spang confirmed Netflix internal metrics showed uncompromised video quality with significant retransmission drops.

