### TCP Segmentation Offload Without Microbursts
* **Presenter:** Sebastian Rust
* **Slides:** [TCP Segmentation Offload Without Microbursts](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iccrg-tcp-segmentation-offload-without-microbursts-01)
* **Discussion:**
  * Sebastian Rust presented a hybrid host-NIC pacing mechanism designed to eliminate microbursts caused by TCP Segmentation Offload (TSO).
  * **Problem:** Software pacing operating above TSO outputs "super-packets" (up to 64 KB). When passed to the NIC, the NIC transmits the constituent segments back-to-back at full line rate, creating microbursts of up to 45 packets that induce queue jitter and packet drops on shallow-buffer switches.
  * **Solution:** The host retains congestion rate calculation and coarse super-packet pacing, appending two metadata fields (Flow ID and Inter-Departure Time) to the TX descriptors. A firmware-based timing wheel on the NIC paces individual segments post-segmentation.
  * **Evaluation:** Implemented on a Netronome Agilio CX 10GbE NIC using ~200 lines of driver/firmware code. Microbursts were eliminated, achieving segment inter-departure times within nanoseconds/microseconds of target rates, saving host CPU compared to software-only segment pacing, and significantly increasing the slow-start exit threshold on shallow queues.
* **Q&A Highlights:**
  * *Extending to QUIC/UDP:* Lars Eggert asked about applying this to UDP/GSO for QUIC. Rust confirmed the NIC-based timing wheel concept applies directly to UDP Generic Segmentation Offload (GSO).
  * *NIC Firmware Accessibility:* Vidhi Goel inquired about hardware support across commercial NICs. Rust noted open firmware accessibility (e.g., Netronome or SmartNICs) is required to program the timing wheel.

